<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n94137" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Σῑληνός</span></b> , ὁ,
			<u>Silenus</u>, companion of Dionysus, <i><span class="en">
			Pi.
			<u>Fr.</u>
			156
		</span></i> (s. v.l.), <i><span class="en">
			Hdt.
			7.26
		</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
			8.138
		</span></i>, etc.; father of the Satyrs, <i><span class="en">
			E.
			<u>Cyc.</u>
			13
		</span></i>,<i><span class="en">
			82
		</span></i>,<i><span class="en">
			269
		</span></i>: the older Satyrs were called <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Σιληνοί</span>, <i><span class="en">
			<u>h.Ven.</u>
			262
		</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
			D.S.
			3.72
		</span></i>; but <i><span class="en">
			S.
		</span></i> was distinguished by prophetic powers, <i><span class="en">
			Ael.
			<u>VH</u>
			3.18
		</span></i>. <div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense3"><b>2.</b>
			<i>a figure of Silenus</i>, used as a casket for precious pieces
			of sculpture, <i><span class="en">
				Pl.
				<u>Smp.</u>
				215a
			</span></i>, <i><span class="en">
				215b
			</span></i>. (Freq. written <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Σειλ-</span>, but
				<span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">Σιλ-</span> in early Inscrr., <i><span class="en">
				<u>IG</u>
				12.51
			</span></i>
			<span class="entity">(v B.C.)</span>, Kretschmer <i><span class="en">
				<u>Griech.Vaseninschr.</u>
				p.132
			</span></i>.)</div>
